doT -- SEQ 480 (Tony, 2026-10-30): Do on the new road, PRE-REGISTERED ON TRUNK before the build.

Do is WHILe with the test after the statement, so its body always runs at least once; break and continue land as in WHILe.
This file is not run alone. pop.sh builds two inputs per row, one process each under an alarm: everything above this file's
`stop();`, then the row on the OLD road (the statement itself) or on the NEW road (the rule called by name, then testOP on
what it handed back, except DO-0, which builds and does not run), then `DO NEXT` and a sentinel. Read by value, the old
road's print as the control. wc and wo are declared, so every row starts from 0 in its own process.

TODAY (2026-10-30, measured): Do has no action, so testOP refuses its label (DO-1..4 print nothing); DO-5's WHILe skips the
unbuilt inner Do and prints 9 9; DO-6's STARt skips it and prints 8.

DO-7 (added SEQ 480, H7): DO-4's continue fires mid-loop, so a continue that skipped the condition still reads 1 3 there --
the next pass tests normally. DO-7 continues on the LAST pass (wc 3), where skipping the test runs the body once more.

